  "step_2": "The verbal report is in German and translates to: 'With 3000€ I have a 25% chance of winning, that seems plausible to me.' The individual focuses only on the 25% probability of winning 3000€ in Lottery B and finds this probability subjectively acceptable or reasonable. There is no mention of Lottery A, no comparison of probabilities or outcomes between lotteries, and no discussion of the distribution of probabilities (e.g., skewness, concentration of likelihood on good vs bad outcomes). The justification centers solely on the perceived plausibility of the 25% chance for 3000€, indicating a focus on one aspect of Lottery B without broader structural analysis. The deliberation appears to center on a single attribute (probability of winning in B) and its subjective acceptability.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires comparing the skewness of both lotteries by evaluating how much probability mass is assigned to favorable versus unfavorable outcomes. This involves assessing and comparing the 20% (A) and 25% (B) probabilities of positive outcomes. However, the individual does not mention any comparison of probability distributions, nor do they reference the concept of skewness, balance of probabilities, or concentration of favorable outcomes. While they do note the 25% chance in Lottery B—which aligns with the data relevant to skewness—their reasoning stops at calling it 'plausible' without invoking a comparative structural rule based on skewness. There is no evidence that they evaluated Lottery A's skewness or compared the degree of left-skewness between lotteries. The justification is based on subjective plausibility, not on a rule-based preference for more left-skewed distributions. Therefore, although part of the relevant data (25% for 3000€) is mentioned, the full informational basis and the rule of the decision reason are not reflected in the report.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

